  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "pzg4gpr",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction to Vitamins & Minerals"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Vitamins are organic compounds required in small quantities for essential metabolic and physiological functions.",
        "Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are stored in the body's fatty tissues and liver, whereas water-soluble vitamins (B-complex, C) must be regularly replenished.",
        "Vitamin D functions like a hormone and is synthesized in human skin upon exposure to sunlight.",
        "Essential minerals like Iron and Iodine play critical roles in hemoglobin synthesis and thyroid regulation respectively.",
        "Deficiencies of vitamins lead to distinct metabolic disorders such as Night Blindness (Vit A), Scurvy (Vit C), Rickets (Vit D), and Beriberi (Vit B1)."
      ],
      "id": "bkpgz9u"
    },
